{"id":642,"date":"2012-05-05T19:17:59","date_gmt":"2012-05-05T19:17:59","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/righteousmind.com\/?p=642"},"modified":"2023-10-19T18:02:42","modified_gmt":"2023-10-19T22:02:42","slug":"talking-with-chris-hayes-and-chris-mooney-about-denial-of-science","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/talking-with-chris-hayes-and-chris-mooney-about-denial-of-science\/","title":{"rendered":"Talking with Chris Hayes and Chris Mooney about denial of science"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>I was on <a href=\"http:\/\/www.msnbc.msn.com\/id\/46979738\/vp\/47304976#47304976\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Up, with Chris Hayes<\/a>, talking with Chris Mooney (author of The Republican Brain), John McWhorter, and Michelle Goldberg, about the psychology of science denial. I think Mooney is summarizing the literature correctly in saying that conservatives are psychologically different from liberals, in ways that feed in to the current denial of science. But I point out that both sides deny science when it contradicts their sacred values. The two sides are not equal nowadays &#8212; the Republican Party is spinning away from reality, as <a href=\"http:\/\/www.washingtonpost.com\/opinions\/lets-just-say-it-the-republicans-are-the-problem\/2012\/04\/27\/gIQAxCVUlT_story.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mann and Ornstein<\/a> argue. But this is a recent development &#8212; not an eternal fact about conservatives, and it is to some degree a <a href=\"http:\/\/www.livescience.com\/19341-conservative-trust-science.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">reaction to the increasing liberalism<\/a> of scientists. I suggest that the best way to reach agreement is by indirect methods, creating trusting relationships first, before letting people discuss and debate across party lines.<br \/>\n<object id=\"msnbc4dea2f\" width=\"420\" height=\"245\" classid=\"clsid:d27cdb6e-ae6d-11cf-96b8-444553540000\" codebase=\"http:\/\/download.macromedi